{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"RECOMMENDATION (analysis only — NOT implemented; this needs a human decision on AC1, and one of the two options is a security-posture change).\n\n## Findings\n\n1. **0707 is the ONLY bedrock-pinned module in the llm_live matrix lane.** The other bedrock-pinned file, tests/external/test_bedrock_cache_live.py, carries NO `_live_llm_ready` sentinel — it is plain `pytest.mark.external` with its own `REBAR_LIVE_BEDROCK=1` opt-in, so it runs in the complement (`external`) job and skips there unless explicitly enabled. It is unaffected.\n\n2. **The precise defect is a disagreement inside 0707, not in the workflow.** The module sets `_live_llm_ready = _live_llm.live_llm_ready()`, and that probe answers 'is the credential present for the provider THIS ARM RESOLVES from the standard model class'. But the module then bypasses model-class resolution entirely and pins `_MODEL = \"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6\"`. On the anthropic arm the probe says READY (an Anthropic key exists) while every cell actually calls Bedrock. A module's readiness sentinel must agree with the model it pins; here it cannot.\n\n3. **Cost dimension:** the module is 2 tests, one of which runs `_TRIALS = 3`. Running it on all three arms triples those billable calls to assert the same thing about the same model.\n\n## Recommended: AC1 option (a), implemented as a provider-aware sentinel\n\nMake 0707's readiness reflect the provider it PINS (bedrock) rather than the arm's configured provider, so it executes on the bedrock arm and skips on anthropic/openai.\n\n- No security-posture change: the OIDC step stays gated to `matrix.provider == 'bedrock'`.\n- Honest coverage: the anthropic arm never claimed to cover Bedrock; skipping is accurate, and the arm still FAILS loudly if its OWN credential is missing (the preflight is untouched).\n- Canary-safe: the all-skip canary fires only when collected>=1 and executed==0. Other llm_live tests still execute on non-bedrock arms, so it stays green-for-the-right-reason.\n- Has in-repo precedent: test_bedrock_cache_live.py already self-gates on a bedrock-specific opt-in.\n- Cheaper: removes duplicate live calls on two arms.\n\n## Against option (b) — un-gating the OIDC step to all arms\n\nThis hands AWS credentials to jobs that do not need them, including the arms that hold third-party API keys (ANTHROPIC_API_KEY / OPENAI_API_KEY), widening the blast radius of the scoped Bedrock CI role for ZERO coverage gain — it would call the same model from three arms and learn the same fact three times. Recommend rejecting unless someone wants bedrock-pinned modules to run everywhere for an independent reason. It is also a change an agent should not make unilaterally.\n\n## Third option worth an explicit rejection\n\nUn-pinning `_MODEL` so the eval follows each arm would turn a single-model regression reproduction into a cross-provider behavioral eval. That is a different test with a different purpose (and 3x the spend); the pin exists to hold the model fixed while reproducing bug 0707. Recommend keeping the pin.\n\n## Regardless of the choice\n\nConsider a guard test asserting that any module carrying `_live_llm_ready` does not hard-pin a provider prefix that contradicts its readiness probe — that invariant is what failed here, and it will fail again the next time someone pins a model in this tier.\n\nNote on the already-landing region fix (Gerrit 1658, ticket 79d6): it stays correct under every option above. Exporting a region on all arms is harmless where unused and is required the moment any module pins bedrock."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786560684606191001,"uuid":"336d3e84-25df-42d5-93d6-511e86307281"}